{
  "abstract": "We prove a fully explicit zero-density estimate with Huxley's exponent on a short range. For $\\sigma\\in[0.755,0.785]$ and $T\\ge T_1$, where $T_1=10^{175000}$ for $\\sigma<0.765$ and $T_1=10^{160000}$ otherwise, the number of zeros of $\\zeta$ with $\\beta\\ge\\sigma$ and $T_1<\\gamma\\le T$ is at most $10^{188}\\,T^{3(1-\\sigma)/(3\\sigma-1)}(\\log T)^{45}$. A corollary bounds $N(\\sigma,T)$ for $\\log_{10}T\\ge182721$. The proof uses Montgomery's mollified detector, a smooth dyadic block lemma that removes the zero-dependent weight at constant cost, an explicit form of Huxley's large-values estimate, explicit coefficient mean squares, and a case analysis over four exponent variables. An appendix derives the finite inequalities that place every detected zero in the certified domain. A box decomposition, re-checked in exact rational arithmetic, verifies the case analysis, and the numerical constants are certified with interval arithmetic and explicit tail bounds. The log power is tracked term by term, and each used term has nonpositive excess, so the bound at $T_1$ holds for all larger $T$. We then compare the result with the explicit applications we examined, and it improves none of them. It beats the explicit Ingham-shape bound of Chourasiya and Simonič only above $10^{6645}$ to $10^{42700}$. At the heights used by a conditional prime-gap threshold ($T\\approx10^{210}$), the whole Huxley–Ingham exponent gap is worth $0.7$ to $4.3$ powers of $\\log T$. At the Korobov–Vinogradov edge, in a model of Cully-Hugill's argument, Bellotti's near-unity bound beats every Huxley-shaped bound whose log power exceeds about $6.2$. This quantifies, for these applications, Bellotti's observation that log powers can outweigh exponent gains. We make no claim about applications or implementations outside those analysed.",
